course: dictionary
lesson: 10867
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Victorian» has a capital V when it names a time. Victorian children, «Victorian cooks» and «Victorian prisons» all point to the years when Queen Victoria ruled Britain, from 1837 to 1901. The clues around the word tell you which meaning to use.
explain: Some words hide a second meaning. Ruby says her teacher's rules were «Victorian», and the rules were strict and old-fashioned. The word can describe how people behaved or what their rules were like, not just a time in history.
explain: Words can also belong to a place. In Australia, Victoria is the name of a state, and «Victorian» means from or to do with that state. If a text is about Melbourne or a Victorian bush walk, check the clues before you pick a meaning.
fact: «Victorian» with a capital V means from the time when Queen Victoria ruled Britain, from 1837 to 1901.
fact: A «Victorian» is a person who lived in that time, and the plural is «Victorians».
fact: «Victorian» can also mean old-fashioned or very strict, like people imagined the Victorians to be.
fact: In Australia, Victoria is a state, and «Victorian» means from or to do with it.
fact: Victorian children often learned poems by heart and recited them standing up.
exercise: «Victorian children often learned poems by heart and recited them standing up.» In this sentence, Victorian means | Very tall | Made of glass | Sung loudly | *From the time when Queen Victoria ruled Britain
exercise: Ruby says her teacher's rules were «Victorian», like something out of a history book. What does Victorian suggest here? | Full of sunshine and fun | Sung loudly | Brand new and made of plastic | *Old-fashioned and very strict
exercise: The Victorians lived in Britain from 1837 to 1901. What is the name for a person who lived in that time? | A treadmill | A whiting | A chimney sweep | *A Victorian
test: «In many Victorian prisons prisoners did hard labour, such as walking for hours on a treadwheel.» What is a treadwheel? | A type of train | A wheel on a bicycle | *A huge turning wheel of steps | A kind of soup
test: Some children worked as chimney sweeps' climbing boys in Victorian times. Which year did a law finally stop this? | 1901 | 1066 | *1875 | 1837
exam: Maya reads that a whiting is a silvery fish from the North Sea, and that «Victorian cooks served it fried». Which meaning of Victorian fits this text? | *From the time when Queen Victoria ruled Britain | Brand new and modern | Old-fashioned and very strict | From or to do with the state of Victoria in Australia
